{
  // ===============================================================================================
  // THE MATERIAL PALETTE (#65). A pattern may write "$shaft" where a block id goes, and it resolves
  // against this map -- overlaid by the band's own -- so one authored scheme paints itself in
  // whatever the depth is built from. See the Room Schemes Manual.
  //
  // Every role here is DERIVED from what this file already said rather than invented: these are the
  // ids that recur across the schemes below. The mud band overrides FOUR of them, which is the whole
  // argument for the feature: four lines repaint a floor.
  //
  // A role resolves at BUILD time against the palette in scope, and a role no palette declares is a
  // load error naming the scheme. Values here are always literal ids -- a role pointing at a role is
  // a cycle nothing checks, and is rejected.
  //
  // NOT every literal below has been converted to a role yet. A scheme that can roll on FLOOR 0
  // meets the mud band's palette there, so converting it would repaint it -- which is a look to
  // choose, not a migration to run. Those schemes still name their blocks outright.
  // ===============================================================================================
  "palette": {
    "shaft": "dungeonblocks:stone_bricks_pillar_block",
    "footing": "dungeonblocks:stone_bricks_pillar_base_block",
    "stair": "minecraft:stone_brick_stairs",
